package cajeta.wire;

/**
 * The allocation-free companion of {@link Encoder}: serialize into a
 * CALLER-owned buffer instead of allocating a fresh one per call
 * (cajeta-llama plan 10.2.4; spec 13.17). Every allocation takes the
 * process-global live-set mutex once a second thread exists, so hot
 * serialization paths — the `LtmPager` writing thousands of entries per
 * flush — need a per-call path that allocates nothing.
 *
 * Deliberately a SEPARATE interface rather than a new method on
 * `Encoder`: `Encoder` is public API with implementers outside this
 * repository, and the allocating form and its callers must stay
 * untouched (10.1.8). A codec that supports both forms implements both
 * interfaces (or ships two small objects); consumers that can exploit
 * the in-place form — `LtmBPlusTree`'s buffer-encoder constructor —
 * accept a `BufferEncoder` alongside the `Encoder`.
 *
 * ## Contract
 *
 * `encodeInto(value, dst, off)` writes `value`'s encoding at
 * `dst[off ..)` and returns the byte count written — the SAME bytes
 * `Encoder.encode` would produce, so the two forms are interchangeable
 * on disk. The caller guarantees capacity; an encoding is bounded by
 * the codec's documented maximum size.
 *
 * ```cajeta
 * int8[] page = heap int8[4096];
 * int32 n = fast.encodeInto(key, page, 64);   // zero allocations
 * ```
 */
public interface BufferEncoder<T> {
    /**
     * Write `value`'s encoding into `dst` at `off`; return the byte
     * count. Must produce byte-identical output to the corresponding
     * `Encoder.encode`.
     *
     * @EntryPoint
     */
    int32 encodeInto(T value, int8[] dst, int32 off);
}
